// NEGATIVE TEST — a guarded `true` does not cover `true` (N6-02 / N6-09).
//
// `bool` is the one scrutinee type literal patterns can exhaust, and this match
// looks complete: `true` and `false` are both written. But the `true` arm carries
// a guard, so it is taken only sometimes and covers nothing — leaving `true` with
// nowhere to go when the guard fails. The bool rule and the guard rule are the
// same rule seen twice, and this is where they meet.
fn main() {
    let b = 1 < 2;
    let v = match b {
        true if 1 > 0 => 1,
        false => 0,
    };
    print_int(v);
}
